as we do more iterations.

252
00:13:37,410 --> 00:13:40,650
And another good thing
to remind ourselves of

253
00:13:40,650 --> 00:13:45,240
is that this is here, this
sort of expected discounted sum

254
00:13:45,240 --> 00:13:48,210
of rewards, what we're
doing in this equation

255
00:13:48,210 --> 00:13:54,120
is we are plugging in this term
as an estimate of the expected

256
00:13:54,120 --> 00:13:57,725
discounted rewards
for the future.

257
00:13:57,725 --> 00:14:00,350
So we're saying, we've got this
estimate of the value function.

258
00:14:00,350 --> 00:14:02,450
We're going to plug-in
and say, my reward

259
00:14:02,450 --> 00:14:04,280
is my immediate reward
plus my discounted

260
00:14:04,280 --> 00:14:05,640
sum of future rewards.

261
00:14:05,640 --> 00:14:07,700
And this is what I'm
using for my discounted

262
00:14:07,700 --> 00:14:09,680
sum of future rewards.

263
00:14:09,680 --> 00:14:12,380
And so this is known as
bootstrapping because we're

264
00:14:12,380 --> 00:14:14,420
plugging in one
estimate in order

265
00:14:14,420 --> 00:14:17,130
to help us do another estimate.

266
00:14:17,130 --> 00:14:20,200
And we'll see a picture
of this graphically later.

267
00:14:20,200 --> 00:14:20,700
All right.

268
00:14:20,700 --> 00:14:23,790
Monte Carlo policy evaluation
is a really simple idea,

269
00:14:23,790 --> 00:14:28,490
but it's very useful
and it is commonly done.

270
00:14:28,490 --> 00:14:31,940
So essentially, the idea with
Monte Carlo policy evaluation

271
00:14:31,940 --> 00:14:35,390
is, we are just going
to simulate or act

272
00:14:35,390 --> 00:14:36,210
in the real world.

273
00:14:36,210 --> 00:14:37,585
So this is just
saying you've got

274
00:14:37,585 --> 00:14:40,800
a policy, which means what
action to take in every state.

275
00:14:40,800 --> 00:14:43,520
Today, we'll mostly focus on
deterministic policies just

276
00:14:43,520 --> 00:14:45,650
to make it easier.

277
00:14:45,650 --> 00:14:53,450
So I'll just say that,
for most of today,

278
00:14:53,450 --> 00:14:57,766
assume pi is deterministic.

279
00:15:00,840 --> 00:15:02,948
But all of these ideas
can easily be extended.

280
00:15:02,948 --> 00:15:04,740
Just easy to write that
down without having

281
00:15:04,740 --> 00:15:07,410
to do an expectation
over actions everywhere.

282
00:15:07,410 --> 00:15:09,580
OK, so what's the
idea in this case?

283
00:15:09,580 --> 00:15:15,050
Well, the value function is just
an average over the returns.

284
00:15:15,050 --> 00:15:17,870
It's an expectation over the
trajectories or the returns

285
00:15:17,870 --> 00:15:21,170
you could get by
following the policy.

286
00:15:21,170 --> 00:15:25,640
And therefore, the value is
just the mean of returns.

287
00:15:25,640 --> 00:15:27,260
And we know how to
approximate means.

288
00:15:27,260 --> 00:15:31,820
We just do things a bunch
of times and we average.

289
00:15:31,820 --> 00:15:34,110
And so as an example of
this, it might be something

290
00:15:34,110 --> 00:15:36,440
that someone says,
OK, I want to know

291
00:15:36,440 --> 00:15:41,030
if we, say, give a particular
set of patient treatments,

292
00:15:41,030 --> 00:15:43,680
and maybe those treatments
take a year, for example.

293
00:15:43,680 --> 00:15:46,650
And someone wants to on
average, how good is that.

294
00:15:46,650 --> 00:15:49,260
Well, what you could do is
you could have 100 patients.

295
00:15:49,260 --> 00:15:52,130
I'll go through that
particular protocol for a year

296
00:15:52,130 --> 00:15:54,470
and then average their outcomes.

297
00:15:54,470 --> 00:15:58,190
And that would be an example of
Monte Carlo policy evaluation,

298
00:15:58,190 --> 00:16:00,500
because you just
execute the policy

299
00:16:00,500 --> 00:16:04,633
for many different episodes,
and then you average.

300
00:16:04,633 --> 00:16:06,050
And one thing just
to note here is

301
00:16:06,050 --> 00:16:09,230
that you can have cases here
where not all the trajectories

302
00:16:09,230 --> 00:16:11,260
are the same length.

303
00:16:11,260 --> 00:16:13,660
So imagine, in the
patient case I just gave,

304
00:16:13,660 --> 00:16:15,340
you might have that
some people drop out

305
00:16:15,340 --> 00:16:16,690
of a trial during the year.

306
00:16:16,690 --> 00:16:19,790
Or maybe they finish their
treatment successfully,

307
00:16:19,790 --> 00:16:21,760
and so then they're also done.

308
00:16:21,760 --> 00:16:24,290
So all the trajectories
may not be the same length,

309
00:16:24,290 --> 00:16:26,950
but essentially, you can
just think of it as you just

310
00:16:26,950 --> 00:16:28,840
have many, many trajectories.

311
00:16:28,840 --> 00:16:31,790
Maybe this one
has a G equals 10.

312
00:16:31,790 --> 00:16:33,410
This had a G equals 5.

313
00:16:33,410 --> 00:16:35,020
This has a G equals 10.

314
00:16:35,020 --> 00:16:36,970
And you just average
over all of them.

315
00:16:36,970 --> 00:16:39,645
And that is your value function.

316
00:16:43,106 --> 00:16:45,850
Now, one of the benefits of
this is that, when we do this,

317
00:16:45,850 --> 00:16:47,280
we're not actually--

318
00:16:47,280 --> 00:16:48,280
benefits or drawbacks.

319
00:16:48,280 --> 00:16:49,510
And we'll talk about this.

320
00:16:49,510 --> 00:16:52,920
This is making no assumption
that the system is a Markov

321
00:16:52,920 --> 00:16:54,130
decision process.

322
00:16:54,130 --> 00:16:55,980
It's just averaging.

323
00:16:55,980 --> 00:17:00,660
So your system
might not be Markov.

324
00:17:00,660 --> 00:17:02,970
What I mean by that is
that you, in general,

325
00:17:02,970 --> 00:17:06,380
will have a finite set of
features to describe the state.

326
00:17:06,380 --> 00:17:08,130
So if we think about
that patient example

327
00:17:08,130 --> 00:17:11,339
I just had, maybe you have
different vitals of the patient.

328
00:17:11,339 --> 00:17:13,680
Maybe you have static
demographic variables.

329
00:17:13,680 --> 00:17:16,323
But we do not have all the
features that are probably

330
00:17:16,323 --> 00:17:18,240
going to describe how
someone's going to react

331
00:17:18,240 --> 00:17:20,040
to a set of treatments.

332
00:17:20,040 --> 00:17:22,589
And so because of that,
you may or may not

333
00:17:22,589 --> 00:17:24,960
think that, in the features
you have access to,

334
00:17:24,960 --> 00:17:27,089
the system is Markov.

335
00:17:27,089 --> 00:17:29,260
But this doesn't require
the state to be Markov.

336
00:17:29,260 --> 00:17:30,610
It's just averaging.

337
00:17:30,610 --> 00:17:34,410
You just roll out your policy
many times and you average.

338
00:17:34,410 --> 00:17:35,850
Now, a really
important thing here

339
00:17:35,850 --> 00:17:39,790
is that it can only be
applied to episodic MDPs.

340
00:17:39,790 --> 00:17:41,150
So what do I mean by that?

341
00:17:41,150 --> 00:17:44,140
I mean your episode has
to end in order for you

342
00:17:44,140 --> 00:17:48,060
to see what the
total return was.

343
00:17:48,060 --> 00:17:51,240
So if you have horizon
lengths or episodes that

344
00:17:51,240 --> 00:17:52,830
last for a year, that's OK.

345
00:17:52,830 --> 00:17:55,112
It's a little bit slow,
but you could do that.

346
00:17:55,112 --> 00:17:56,820
But if you want to
just think of how good

347
00:17:56,820 --> 00:18:00,150
a policy is, if you just are
going to act forever and never

348
00:18:00,150 --> 00:18:03,560
stop, this wouldn't
work, or not without

349
00:18:03,560 --> 00:18:07,093
some additional approximations.

350
00:18:07,093 --> 00:18:08,760
Somebody have any
questions about either

351
00:18:08,760 --> 00:18:10,927
of these two things, about
it not assuming the state

352
00:18:10,927 --> 00:18:11,740
is Markov?

353
00:18:11,740 --> 00:18:12,700
Yeah.

354
00:18:12,700 --> 00:18:14,950
You were talking about
medical treatments.

355
00:18:14,950 --> 00:18:19,320
So does this only work
if the treatment only

356
00:18:19,320 --> 00:18:22,480
lasts the same amount of
time for every patient,

357
00:18:22,480 --> 00:18:24,030
like six months?

358
00:18:24,030 --> 00:18:26,320
Because they have
different lengths.

359
00:18:26,320 --> 00:18:28,230
How can that be episodic?

360
00:18:28,230 --> 00:18:28,900
Great question.

361
00:18:28,900 --> 00:18:30,852
Yeah, so what [INAUDIBLE]
is like, well,

362
00:18:30,852 --> 00:18:33,310
could it be episodic if the
episodes are different lengths?

363
00:18:33,310 --> 00:18:33,990
It could be.

364
00:18:33,990 --> 00:18:36,430
So it could be that you
have a fixed policy.

365
00:18:36,430 --> 00:18:38,970
And maybe that policy says,
if someone doesn't respond

366
00:18:38,970 --> 00:18:41,280
to this type of treatment,
we do this additional type

367
00:18:41,280 --> 00:18:41,830
of treatment.

368
00:18:41,830 --> 00:18:43,680
In a clinic, that's very common.

369
00:18:43,680 --> 00:18:47,165
As long as the episode
is guaranteed to end,

370
00:18:47,165 --> 00:18:49,770
you know the treatment could
only last, say for a year total,

371
00:18:49,770 --> 00:18:51,990
you can still average
over all those outcomes.

372
00:18:51,990 --> 00:18:55,980
You just sum over the return
for those different ones.

373
00:18:55,980 --> 00:18:57,060
Yeah.

374
00:18:57,060 --> 00:19:00,750
For each of these
trajectories, are we

375
00:19:00,750 --> 00:19:03,540
supposed to begin with
this different state?

376
00:19:03,540 --> 00:19:05,880
Or we can actually start
with the same state?

377
00:19:05,880 --> 00:19:06,710
Great question.

378
00:19:06,710 --> 00:19:09,740
So if we want to get a value
function for all states,

379
00:19:09,740 --> 00:19:12,250
we need to see all the states
inside of these trajectories.

380
00:19:12,250 --> 00:19:14,542
And we'll talk about how we
estimate these in a second.

381
00:19:14,542 --> 00:19:17,170
So I think this
will be answered.

382
00:19:17,170 --> 00:19:19,940
So for example, how
might we compute this?

383
00:19:19,940 --> 00:19:23,590
So we would like to get the
value for all the states

384
00:19:23,590 --> 00:19:25,900
that are reachable
inside of your policy.

385
00:19:25,900 --> 00:19:28,630
So what you could do
is, we can initialize

386
00:19:28,630 --> 00:19:29,750
two different variables.

387
00:19:29,750 --> 00:19:33,280
One, N of s is just going to be
the counts, the number of times

388
00:19:33,280 --> 00:19:36,550
that we've updated our
estimate for state s.

389
00:19:36,550 --> 00:19:40,450
G of s here is going to start
with 0, which we've never seen

390
00:19:40,450 --> 00:19:42,760
any returns from this state.

391
00:19:42,760 --> 00:19:46,730
So what every visit Monte Carlo
does is it samples an episode.

392
00:19:46,730 --> 00:19:50,740
So this goes up to
some time step T, TI.

393
00:19:50,740 --> 00:19:52,360
And this has to
be finite, but it

394
00:19:52,360 --> 00:19:55,850
could be different on
different episodes.

395
00:19:55,850 --> 00:19:58,060
And then we compute
the discounted sum

396
00:19:58,060 --> 00:20:00,680
of rewards for that episode.

397
00:20:00,680 --> 00:20:05,710
OK, so starting at time
step T, how much reward

398
00:20:05,710 --> 00:20:08,130
do we get till the
end of the episode?

399
00:20:08,130 --> 00:20:10,720
And then for every time
step until the end,

400
00:20:10,720 --> 00:20:14,050
we see, is this is the first
time that state's been visited.

401
00:20:14,050 --> 00:20:16,200
Then we update the
total number of times

402
00:20:16,200 --> 00:20:20,310
we visited this state for the
first time per in each episode.

403
00:20:20,310 --> 00:20:22,990
We increment the total
return and we average.

404
00:20:25,710 --> 00:20:30,120
So it steps along, and it just
says, OK, maybe the first time

405
00:20:30,120 --> 00:20:32,400
I reached-- if you think
of the Mars Rover example,

406
00:20:32,400 --> 00:20:33,483
I have it in the next one.

407
00:20:33,483 --> 00:20:35,490
No, I think I put it--
so for a lot of today

408
00:20:35,490 --> 00:20:37,200
I've moved a lot of
the worked examples

409
00:20:37,200 --> 00:20:38,470
till the end of the slides.

410
00:20:38,470 --> 00:20:41,940
But if you want to go through
them later, I encourage you to.

411
00:20:41,940 --> 00:20:44,140
So for example, in the
case of the Mars Rover,

412
00:20:44,140 --> 00:20:46,590
you might imagine you
start in state S3.

413
00:20:46,590 --> 00:20:50,310
And then on that particular
one, you get a reward of 1

414
00:20:50,310 --> 00:20:51,580
for that episode.

415
00:20:51,580 --> 00:20:55,650
And so then you would average
in 1, starting in that state S3

416
00:20:55,650 --> 00:20:56,345
till the end.

417
00:21:00,360 --> 00:21:03,160
So this is first visit
Monte Carlo evaluation,

418
00:21:03,160 --> 00:21:05,220
which means you only
update a state at most

419
00:21:05,220 --> 00:21:07,130
once in each episode.

420
00:21:07,130 --> 00:21:12,840
So if you had something like
this, S1 went to S2, went to S3,

421
00:21:12,840 --> 00:21:15,940
went to S2, went to S3.

422
00:21:15,940 --> 00:21:21,870
So in this case, dot, dot,
dot, you would update S1 once

423
00:21:21,870 --> 00:21:24,660
in this trajectory, and
you would update S2 once,

424
00:21:24,660 --> 00:21:28,260
and S3 once, even though you,
in fact, visit those states

425
00:21:28,260 --> 00:21:29,140
multiple times.

426
00:21:29,140 --> 00:21:32,240
You only update them for
the first time you visit.

427
00:21:32,240 --> 00:21:33,330
Yeah.

428
00:21:33,330 --> 00:21:37,380
So this have the problem
where the state is really

429
00:21:37,380 --> 00:21:41,490
rare or uncommon, then we
get a really bad [INAUDIBLE]

430
00:21:41,490 --> 00:21:44,010
or we just never
visit it at all?

431
00:21:44,010 --> 00:21:47,110
So if you don't ever visit
a state under the policy,

432
00:21:47,110 --> 00:21:49,360
that's OK, because then you
don't have a value for it.

433
00:21:49,360 --> 00:21:51,402
But then it's kind of
undefined because you never

434
00:21:51,402 --> 00:21:53,850
would reach it.

435
00:21:53,850 --> 00:21:55,750
In this case, as
[INAUDIBLE] says,

436
00:21:55,750 --> 00:21:58,330
if there's a state that's
really rare for you

437
00:21:58,330 --> 00:22:00,340
to reach inside
of your policy, it

438
00:22:00,340 --> 00:22:02,260
might take a lot of
trajectories in order

439
00:22:02,260 --> 00:22:04,090
to get a good estimate of it.

440
00:22:04,090 --> 00:22:07,970
So maybe there's some rare side
effect of a treatment plan,

441
00:22:07,970 --> 00:22:11,288
and it's going to take a lot of
trajectories to observe that.

442
00:22:11,288 --> 00:22:13,330
And that was one of the
challenges with the COVID

443
00:22:13,330 --> 00:22:16,030
vaccine, is that, of course, it
was a finite number of people.

444
00:22:16,030 --> 00:22:18,350
It was a pretty large
number, but pretty finite.

445
00:22:18,350 --> 00:22:20,110
And some side
effects don't show up

446
00:22:20,110 --> 00:22:22,478
until you get many, many more.

447
00:22:22,478 --> 00:22:24,020
It's true, generally,
for treatments,

448
00:22:24,020 --> 00:22:25,600
even if on average
they're totally fine.

449
00:22:25,600 --> 00:22:27,642
But you won't see some of
those rare side effects

450
00:22:27,642 --> 00:22:30,130
until you get an enormous
number of trajectories.

451
00:22:30,130 --> 00:22:32,440
Now, COVID vaccine
certainly had--

452
00:22:32,440 --> 00:22:35,480
the benefits there way
outweigh side effects.

453
00:22:35,480 --> 00:22:37,510
But my point is
just to highlight

454
00:22:37,510 --> 00:22:40,100
that, depending on how
frequently you see the states,

455
00:22:40,100 --> 00:22:42,850
it will take you more or less
a number of total episodes

456
00:22:42,850 --> 00:22:44,240
in order to observe.

457
00:22:44,240 --> 00:22:44,840
Yeah.

458
00:22:44,840 --> 00:22:48,880
Is it fine if it doesn't matter
that I saw S2 again or S3 again

459
00:22:48,880 --> 00:22:49,990
in this trajectory?

460
00:22:49,990 --> 00:22:52,190
For this algorithm, no.

461
00:22:52,190 --> 00:22:53,860
It probably affects
the reward still.

462
00:22:53,860 --> 00:22:55,540
It's just that you
don't use that data.

463
00:22:55,540 --> 00:22:58,480
So an alternative is
called every visit, where

464
00:22:58,480 --> 00:23:01,230
every time you see the state in
that trajectory, you update it.

465
00:23:04,302 --> 00:23:06,010
And as you might
imagine there, let's say

466
00:23:06,010 --> 00:23:08,200
you see it's a really
long trajectory,

467
00:23:08,200 --> 00:23:09,835
and you see S too many times.

468
00:23:09,835 --> 00:23:11,460
Then you would update
for all of those.

469
00:23:14,326 --> 00:23:17,330
So I'm just going to show you
three common different ones.

470
00:23:17,330 --> 00:23:20,620
So this is a worked example
you can go through later

471
00:23:20,620 --> 00:23:24,290
if you want, which is, if you
imagine this is the Mars Rover,

472
00:23:24,290 --> 00:23:25,970
the rewards are on either side.

473
00:23:25,970 --> 00:23:27,490
This is the
particular trajectory.

474
00:23:27,490 --> 00:23:30,310
You can compute the first visit
and the every visit Monte Carlo

475
00:23:30,310 --> 00:23:32,620
estimates.

476
00:23:32,620 --> 00:23:35,310
So both of those are totally
reasonable things to do.

477
00:23:35,310 --> 00:23:37,750
Perhaps more common is what's
known as incremental Monte

478
00:23:37,750 --> 00:23:39,280
Carlo.

479
00:23:39,280 --> 00:23:41,630
And this does kind of what
you would expect it to do,

480
00:23:41,630 --> 00:23:44,260
which is you maintain a
running estimate for what

481
00:23:44,260 --> 00:23:47,210
is the value under a policy
for a particular state,

482
00:23:47,210 --> 00:23:51,310
and you smoothly update
that as you get more data.

483
00:23:51,310 --> 00:23:53,530
So what we would
do in this case is

484
00:23:53,530 --> 00:23:57,220
you keep track of the number of
times you visited that state,

485
00:23:57,220 --> 00:24:00,370
and then you weigh
your old estimate

486
00:24:00,370 --> 00:24:03,100
by the number of times you've
visit the state minus 1 divided

487
00:24:03,100 --> 00:24:07,720
by N s, plus your new return you
just observed divided by N s.

488
00:24:07,720 --> 00:24:09,790
So that's just sort
of your way you're

489
00:24:09,790 --> 00:24:13,570
kind of constantly updating your
value function for this state

490
00:24:13,570 --> 00:24:16,250
as you get more data.

491
00:24:16,250 --> 00:24:18,713
And for those of you who have
done machine learning, which

492
00:24:18,713 --> 00:24:21,130
is probably most of you, this
should look pretty familiar.

493
00:24:21,130 --> 00:24:22,672
This is kind of like
a learning rate.

494
00:24:25,320 --> 00:24:29,960
This is your updated value,
and this is your old value.

495
00:24:29,960 --> 00:24:32,490
And in fact, that's what
we're going to see here.

496
00:24:32,490 --> 00:24:33,810
OK?

497
00:24:33,810 --> 00:24:35,520
So you can think
of this in general.

498
00:24:35,520 --> 00:24:39,470
It doesn't have to be 1 over N.
It can just be any alpha here.

499
00:24:39,470 --> 00:24:45,670
So any sort of alpha here
is just a learning rate.

500
00:24:45,670 --> 00:24:48,010
And we're just smoothly
updating our estimate

501
00:24:48,010 --> 00:24:50,760
of what is the value function
for a particular state.

502
00:24:54,600 --> 00:24:57,160
And we'll see lots, lots and
lots of algorithms like this,

503
00:24:57,160 --> 00:25:01,240
similar to probably what
you saw in machine learning.

504
00:25:01,240 --> 00:25:04,280
The key thing here is the
estimates that we're using,

505
00:25:04,280 --> 00:25:08,380
which we also might use
the reward targets often.

506
00:25:08,380 --> 00:25:13,440
We have this estimate here, and
then we have our old estimate.

507
00:25:13,440 --> 00:25:16,310
So this is one sample
of what is the return,

508
00:25:16,310 --> 00:25:18,660
starting in this state till
the end of the episode.

509
00:25:22,007 --> 00:25:24,340
So I think it's helpful to
think a little bit about what

510
00:25:24,340 --> 00:25:25,790
this looks like pictorially.

511
00:25:25,790 --> 00:25:27,670
And this also relates a lot to--

512
00:25:27,670 --> 00:25:29,955
we'll see this when we talk
about things like AlphaGo.

513
00:25:32,600 --> 00:25:33,890
So I think it's helpful today.

514
00:25:33,890 --> 00:25:35,600
And it'll look somewhat
familiar to you

515
00:25:35,600 --> 00:25:38,540
if you've seen things like
minimax trees or expectimax

516
00:25:38,540 --> 00:25:39,380
trees.

517
00:25:39,380 --> 00:25:43,161
Who here has seen
expectimax trees before?

518
00:25:43,161 --> 00:25:44,407
OK, so maybe one person.

519
00:25:44,407 --> 00:25:45,240
So most people, not.

520
00:25:45,240 --> 00:25:48,750
So this might be a
useful representation.

521
00:25:48,750 --> 00:25:50,660
So I think one way to
think about this is,

522
00:25:50,660 --> 00:25:52,400
what we're trying to
do is we're trying

523
00:25:52,400 --> 00:25:55,790
to think of what the value is
starting in a certain state.

524
00:25:55,790 --> 00:25:59,000
And we know what
the action is we're

525
00:25:59,000 --> 00:26:00,930
going to take because
we've got to fix policy.

526
00:26:00,930 --> 00:26:02,690
So that says we start
in this state s.

527
00:26:02,690 --> 00:26:04,480
We take this action a.

528
00:26:04,480 --> 00:26:06,540
This is prescribed
by our policy,

529
00:26:06,540 --> 00:26:11,750
so this is pi of s
is going to equal.

530
00:26:11,750 --> 00:26:14,060
And then after we do that,
because the world might

531
00:26:14,060 --> 00:26:16,640
be stochastic, we're going to
have a bunch of next states

532
00:26:16,640 --> 00:26:18,110
we could reach.

533
00:26:18,110 --> 00:26:23,810
So we have probability of
s prime, given s and a.

534
00:26:23,810 --> 00:26:27,560
And what we're trying to do
when we do policy evaluation is

535
00:26:27,560 --> 00:26:29,630
we're trying to get an
expectation over all

536
00:26:29,630 --> 00:26:31,340
the potential futures
we might end up

537
00:26:31,340 --> 00:26:33,380
in by following this policy.

538
00:26:33,380 --> 00:26:36,143
Maybe in some cases, there's
really good patient outcomes,

539
00:26:36,143 --> 00:26:37,310
hopefully, most of the time.

540
00:26:37,310 --> 00:26:39,643
And maybe sometimes, there's
less good patient outcomes.

541
00:26:39,643 --> 00:26:42,290
And we want to do an
expectation over all of this.

542
00:26:42,290 --> 00:26:43,920
So we can think
of that as a tree,

543
00:26:43,920 --> 00:26:46,410
as just, we start in a
state, we take an action,

544
00:26:46,410 --> 00:26:49,170
we look at the branching factor
of all possible next states,

545
00:26:49,170 --> 00:26:51,590
and then we repeat.

546
00:26:51,590 --> 00:26:53,300
Excuse me.

547
00:26:53,300 --> 00:26:55,610
So if we think of what the
policy evaluation diagram is

548
00:26:55,610 --> 00:26:59,700
doing, for each state, we know
what the next action is we take.

549
00:26:59,700 --> 00:27:03,020
And then we branch again
in terms of states.

550
00:27:03,020 --> 00:27:06,295
So this is like s prime,
and this is s double prime.

551
00:27:13,280 --> 00:27:16,890
And so we can just think of this
tree of possibilities going out.

552
00:27:16,890 --> 00:27:19,370
But we don't have any there's
no branching on the actions

553
00:27:19,370 --> 00:27:22,778
because the actions are
fixed by our policy.

554
00:27:22,778 --> 00:27:25,320
So then if we go all the way
out, and then what we want to do

555
00:27:25,320 --> 00:27:27,640
is we want to figure out
what the value function is,

556
00:27:27,640 --> 00:27:29,670
you can think of this
as an expensive way

557
00:27:29,670 --> 00:27:31,440
to do dynamic programming.

558
00:27:31,440 --> 00:27:33,030
What you would do
is you would take

559
00:27:33,030 --> 00:27:35,550
an expectation over the
states, and then you

560
00:27:35,550 --> 00:27:39,570
would propagate those
values back up to the root.

561
00:27:39,570 --> 00:27:41,850
And if you don't find this
a useful conceptual way

562
00:27:41,850 --> 00:27:43,320
to think about it, it's fine.

563
00:27:43,320 --> 00:27:44,640
I think it can be
helpful to then think

564
00:27:44,640 --> 00:27:46,598
about what these different
algorithms are doing

565
00:27:46,598 --> 00:27:48,510
in terms of approximations.

566
00:27:48,510 --> 00:27:53,808
So this is what we would like
to do in order to get v pi of s.

567
00:27:53,808 --> 00:27:55,350
But we want to do
this in a much more

568
00:27:55,350 --> 00:28:00,920
computationally efficient way,
and also, sample efficient way.

569
00:28:00,920 --> 00:28:04,190
So what Monte Carlo policy
evaluation is going to do

570
00:28:04,190 --> 00:28:07,340
is it's going to look like
that particular equation here.

571
00:28:07,340 --> 00:28:09,860
And what it is
doing here is it is

572
00:28:09,860 --> 00:28:17,800
going to approximate these
full expectations via sample.

573
00:28:17,800 --> 00:28:20,242
So in particular,
what it's doing here

574
00:28:20,242 --> 00:28:21,700
is it's updating
the value estimate

575
00:28:21,700 --> 00:28:26,356
by using a sample of the return
to approximate an expectation.

576
00:28:28,980 --> 00:28:30,240
And we do this many times.

577
00:28:30,240 --> 00:28:31,980
We average over
many such returns.

578
00:28:31,980 --> 00:28:33,480
So it's kind of
like saying you have

579
00:28:33,480 --> 00:28:35,200
this enormous branching tree.

580
00:28:35,200 --> 00:28:38,460
You could do an expectation
over all of that explicitly up

581
00:28:38,460 --> 00:28:40,830
from the roots, or you could
just sample many times,

582
00:28:40,830 --> 00:28:43,560
and that's also going
to approximate the tree.

583
00:28:43,560 --> 00:28:45,240
And the more samples
you get, the better

584
00:28:45,240 --> 00:28:48,970
that's going to be as an
approximation of the tree.

585
00:28:48,970 --> 00:28:52,330
And this type of idea has been
used in many different types

586
00:28:52,330 --> 00:28:53,200
of algorithms.

587
00:28:53,200 --> 00:28:56,920
There's some really nice work in
the mid 2000s by Michael Kearns

588
00:28:56,920 --> 00:28:57,700
and others.

589
00:28:57,700 --> 00:28:59,410
And then similar
ideas were really

590
00:28:59,410 --> 00:29:01,060
the foundation that
then led to some

591
00:29:01,060 --> 00:29:03,250
of the advances of
Monte Carlo tree search,

592
00:29:03,250 --> 00:29:06,670
and then that went into AlphaGo.

593
00:29:06,670 --> 00:29:10,690
So this is what Monte
Carlo tree search is doing.

594
00:29:10,690 --> 00:29:13,870
So notice, it's not doing
any form of bootstrapping.

595
00:29:13,870 --> 00:29:16,070
There's no dynamic programming
that's going on here.

596
00:29:16,070 --> 00:29:17,330
It's just rolling out.

597
00:29:17,330 --> 00:29:25,666
And then what we have here
is, it is using this here,

598
00:29:25,666 --> 00:29:29,770
this sample as an approximation.

599
00:29:29,770 --> 00:29:31,370
OK?

600
00:29:31,370 --> 00:29:31,870
All right.

601
00:29:31,870 --> 00:29:34,360
So that's how Monte Carlo
policy evaluation works.

602
00:29:34,360 --> 00:29:35,790
One natural question
in this case

603
00:29:35,790 --> 00:29:37,227
is, how good is that estimate.

604
00:29:37,227 --> 00:29:39,060
So we're going to see
lots of different ways

605
00:29:39,060 --> 00:29:42,400
and lots of algorithms for
trying to do policy evaluation.

606
00:29:42,400 --> 00:29:45,580
And so you might now ask,
well, how do I pick among them.

607
00:29:45,580 --> 00:29:47,910
What are the properties
I should think about?

608
00:29:47,910 --> 00:29:50,730
So one pretty basic
property that you might want

609
00:29:50,730 --> 00:29:53,250
is consistency, which
means that, as you

610
00:29:53,250 --> 00:29:56,280
get more and more data,
does your estimate actually

611
00:29:56,280 --> 00:30:03,473
converge to the true value of
the policy for all the states.

612
00:30:03,473 --> 00:30:04,890
And this is something
you probably

613
00:30:04,890 --> 00:30:07,350
want in many cases, at
least, because otherwise, it

614
00:30:07,350 --> 00:30:09,550
means that, even if
you had infinite data,

615
00:30:09,550 --> 00:30:11,303
your estimate is still
going to be wrong.

616
00:30:11,303 --> 00:30:13,720
Now, as we start to think about
more complicated settings,

617
00:30:13,720 --> 00:30:19,510
we might have to be satisfied
with this less good objective.

618
00:30:19,510 --> 00:30:22,260
But here, for right
now, we're hoping

619
00:30:22,260 --> 00:30:24,450
we can just write down
the value of every state

620
00:30:24,450 --> 00:30:26,280
as an entry in a
table that we should

621
00:30:26,280 --> 00:30:28,650
be able to get consistency.

622
00:30:28,650 --> 00:30:31,980
A second thing we might want
is computational efficiency.

623
00:30:31,980 --> 00:30:35,010
We'd like this not to be too
expensive for us to compute.

624
00:30:35,010 --> 00:30:38,190
We'd like us not to
require too much memory.

625
00:30:38,190 --> 00:30:42,630
And we'd like it to have
statistical efficiency, which

626
00:30:42,630 --> 00:30:45,720
is, essentially, how does the
accuracy of the estimate change

627
00:30:45,720 --> 00:30:48,390
with the amount of data.

628
00:30:48,390 --> 00:30:50,440
And what that means
here is, more formally,

629
00:30:50,440 --> 00:30:53,490
we'd like to know how quickly
do these things converge

630
00:30:53,490 --> 00:30:56,130
as you get more and more data.

631
00:30:56,130 --> 00:30:59,160
And then in reality, we often
care about empirical accuracy,

632
00:30:59,160 --> 00:31:01,920
just what is our mean
squared error for our types

633
00:31:01,920 --> 00:31:03,630
of our estimators.

634
00:31:03,630 --> 00:31:05,590
So how good is Monte Carlo?

635
00:31:05,590 --> 00:31:08,730
Well, let's just first
quickly remind ourselves

636
00:31:08,730 --> 00:31:11,260
that the bias of an
estimator is this.

637
00:31:11,260 --> 00:31:13,140
So if we have an
estimator theta, which

638
00:31:13,140 --> 00:31:16,840
we're going to be thinking
of as our value function

639
00:31:16,840 --> 00:31:20,130
approximation, it's going to
be the difference between,

640
00:31:20,130 --> 00:31:23,370
on average, what our estimator
is versus the true value.

641
00:31:23,370 --> 00:31:25,740
That's our bias.

642
00:31:25,740 --> 00:31:28,170
And the variance of an
estimator is the difference

643
00:31:28,170 --> 00:31:31,210
between this and its
expectation squared,

644
00:31:31,210 --> 00:31:32,562
the expectation of that.

645
00:31:32,562 --> 00:31:34,020
And the mean squared
error is going

646
00:31:34,020 --> 00:31:35,525
to be variance
plus bias squared.

647
00:31:38,752 --> 00:31:40,460
So generally, you
would like an estimator

648
00:31:40,460 --> 00:31:42,043
that has low mean
squared error, which

649
00:31:42,043 --> 00:31:44,665
means we want it to have low
or zero bias and low variance.

650
00:31:47,640 --> 00:31:50,140
Something to think about if
you're less familiar with these,

651
00:31:50,140 --> 00:31:53,260
is whether or not if an
estimator is unbiased,

652
00:31:53,260 --> 00:31:54,900
is it consistent.

653
00:31:54,900 --> 00:31:58,750
It is not necessarily
consistent, just so you know.

654
00:31:58,750 --> 00:32:02,910
So what we would like here
is that, asymptotically,

655
00:32:02,910 --> 00:32:05,195
the probability that
our estimator-- so N

656
00:32:05,195 --> 00:32:07,320
here is the amount of data
we're using to construct

657
00:32:07,320 --> 00:32:10,650
that estimator-- the
probability that, as we get

658
00:32:10,650 --> 00:32:13,440
an infinite amount of
data, that our estimate is

659
00:32:13,440 --> 00:32:16,360
different than the true
value by more than epsilon.

660
00:32:16,360 --> 00:32:17,240
It has to go to 0.

661
00:32:19,850 --> 00:32:22,580
OK, so we would like
it to be consistent.

662
00:32:22,580 --> 00:32:26,660
So how does Monte Carlo fare
on these sort of properties?

663
00:32:26,660 --> 00:32:31,810
Well, first visit is unbiased.

664
00:32:31,810 --> 00:32:34,100
So it's an unbiased
estimate of the true policy.

665
00:32:34,100 --> 00:32:36,880
And by the law of large
numbers, as the amount of data

666
00:32:36,880 --> 00:32:39,710
you have goes to
infinity per state,

667
00:32:39,710 --> 00:32:41,600
so if you have
really rare states,

668
00:32:41,600 --> 00:32:43,560
you're still going to
need a number of samples

669
00:32:43,560 --> 00:32:44,630
to estimate them.

670
00:32:44,630 --> 00:32:46,880
But as the amount of data
you have goes to infinity,

671
00:32:46,880 --> 00:32:47,930
you'll converge.

672
00:32:47,930 --> 00:32:51,800
So it's consistent
and it's unbiased.

673
00:32:51,800 --> 00:32:55,070
Every visit Monte
Carlo is biased.

674
00:32:55,070 --> 00:32:58,680
One way to think about
that is, in the first case,

675
00:32:58,680 --> 00:33:01,340
all your data is IID,
independent and identically

676
00:33:01,340 --> 00:33:04,680
distributed, in that
every visit case--

677
00:33:04,680 --> 00:33:08,690
imagine that you visit state s2,
and then four steps later, you

678
00:33:08,690 --> 00:33:10,340
visit s2.

679
00:33:10,340 --> 00:33:13,370
Well, their returns are going
to be correlated because they're

680
00:33:13,370 --> 00:33:17,155
both in the same trajectory,
so they're not IID anymore.

681
00:33:17,155 --> 00:33:19,530
So that's just some intuition
for why it might be biased.

682
00:33:19,530 --> 00:33:21,170
But it's also
consistent and it often

683
00:33:21,170 --> 00:33:23,000
has better mean squared
error because you

684
00:33:23,000 --> 00:33:26,480
get to use more of your data
inside of a single trajectory

685
00:33:26,480 --> 00:33:28,280
to do more updates.

686
00:33:28,280 --> 00:33:30,320
And then incremental
Monte Carlo methods

687
00:33:30,320 --> 00:33:35,520
depend on the learning
rate, as you might expect.

688
00:33:35,520 --> 00:33:38,110
So see that here?

689
00:33:38,110 --> 00:33:40,950
So let's imagine
that we are going

690
00:33:40,950 --> 00:33:43,620
to have our alpha parameter,
which is our learning

691
00:33:43,620 --> 00:33:45,880
rate, which is trading off
between our new estimate

692
00:33:45,880 --> 00:33:48,340
and our old estimate.

693
00:33:48,340 --> 00:33:50,283
It can actually
change per time step.

694
00:33:50,283 --> 00:33:51,700
So just like how
you can generally

695
00:33:51,700 --> 00:33:53,590
decay your learning rate, you
can change your learning rate

696
00:33:53,590 --> 00:33:54,340
here.

697
00:33:54,340 --> 00:33:56,260
And if your learning
rate is such

698
00:33:56,260 --> 00:33:59,600
that if you sum up all of its
values for a particular state,

699
00:33:59,600 --> 00:34:02,650
it goes to infinity, but the
square is less than infinity,

700
00:34:02,650 --> 00:34:05,100
then you will converge
to the true value.

701
00:34:08,520 --> 00:34:13,350
And again, these are pretty
common types of criteria

702
00:34:13,350 --> 00:34:14,850
we'll see for some
of the algorithms

703
00:34:14,850 --> 00:34:17,860
we have that, under
some sort of smoothness,

704
00:34:17,860 --> 00:34:20,563
guarantees for the
learning rates we'll

705
00:34:20,563 --> 00:34:21,730
have some decent properties.

706
00:34:21,730 --> 00:34:23,909
Yeah, remind me of your name.

707
00:34:23,909 --> 00:34:27,455
If those conditions aren't
met, do you definitely

708
00:34:27,455 --> 00:34:29,580
not have a guarantee, or
are there other conditions

709
00:34:29,580 --> 00:34:31,622
that can give you a
guarantee, and those are just

710
00:34:31,622 --> 00:34:32,790
some other queries?

711
00:34:32,790 --> 00:34:33,550
Great question.

712
00:34:33,550 --> 00:34:39,330
So he's asking, is it required
to have these conditions.

713
00:34:39,330 --> 00:34:41,100
These are sufficient.

714
00:34:41,100 --> 00:34:43,330
They aren't necessary always.

715
00:34:43,330 --> 00:34:46,330
A lot of that will depend on the
particular problem domain, too,

716
00:34:46,330 --> 00:34:48,306
and what the dynamics
and the reward is.

717
00:34:48,306 --> 00:34:49,889
To my knowledge, I'm
not sure if there

718
00:34:49,889 --> 00:34:52,300
are other really general
conditions like that,

719
00:34:52,300 --> 00:34:54,389
but there might be for
specific problem classes.

720
00:34:54,389 --> 00:34:55,264
It's a good question.

721
00:34:59,463 --> 00:35:00,880
Now, one of the
problems with this

722
00:35:00,880 --> 00:35:04,300
is that, in general, it's a
pretty high variance estimator.

723
00:35:04,300 --> 00:35:07,690
So you're kind of getting,
certainly, with every visit,

724
00:35:07,690 --> 00:35:11,990
or certainly, for first
visit Monte Carlo,

725
00:35:11,990 --> 00:35:15,060
you're only updating the state
at most once per episode.

726
00:35:15,060 --> 00:35:16,340
So it can take a long time.

727
00:35:16,340 --> 00:35:20,020
So you can imagine that, if you
have very different outcomes

728
00:35:20,020 --> 00:35:24,100
from the same starting state,
so maybe most of the time,

729
00:35:24,100 --> 00:35:25,930
you have pretty average
outcomes, but maybe

730
00:35:25,930 --> 00:35:28,570
one in 100 times you have
a really bad outcome.

731
00:35:28,570 --> 00:35:31,660
It's going to take a long time
for that estimator to converge.

732
00:35:31,660 --> 00:35:34,820
So in general, this is a
pretty high variance estimator,

733
00:35:34,820 --> 00:35:39,370
even though it is often
unbiased and it is consistent.

734
00:35:39,370 --> 00:35:40,900
And then the other
big requirement

735
00:35:40,900 --> 00:35:42,860
is that it requires
episodic settings.

736
00:35:42,860 --> 00:35:45,310
So you have to wait till
the end of the episode

737
00:35:45,310 --> 00:35:47,590
to update your estimate.

738
00:35:47,590 --> 00:35:50,150
And for here right now, that
might not seem that bad.

739
00:35:50,150 --> 00:35:53,050
But when we start getting into
control and decision making,

740
00:35:53,050 --> 00:35:56,020
you might want to use the data
you have already in that episode

741
00:35:56,020 --> 00:35:59,140
to change the
behavior of the agent.

742
00:35:59,140 --> 00:36:01,390
So you can imagine
something like, if you're

743
00:36:01,390 --> 00:36:04,790
doing self driving
cars or something,

744
00:36:04,790 --> 00:36:07,300
you're already getting some
evidence that the car is not

745
00:36:07,300 --> 00:36:10,120
working as expected within
a single episode, that

746
00:36:10,120 --> 00:36:10,995
might be really long.

747
00:36:10,995 --> 00:36:12,578
You might want to
use that information

748
00:36:12,578 --> 00:36:14,475
to change how you're
steering, for example.

749
00:36:17,800 --> 00:36:18,300
All right.

750
00:36:18,300 --> 00:36:20,000
So just to summarize
here, what it does

751
00:36:20,000 --> 00:36:22,438
is it's not using
the Markov process.

752
00:36:22,438 --> 00:36:24,230
It's updating your
value function estimate,

753
00:36:24,230 --> 00:36:27,810
using a sample of the return
to approximate the expectation.

754
00:36:27,810 --> 00:36:29,670
And under some pretty
mild conditions,

755
00:36:29,670 --> 00:36:33,730
it converges to the
true value of the state.

756
00:36:33,730 --> 00:36:36,460
And in some cases,
it will turn out

757
00:36:36,460 --> 00:36:38,650
that, even if you actually
know the true dynamics

758
00:36:38,650 --> 00:36:40,733
model and reward, you might
still want to do this.

759
00:36:43,058 --> 00:36:45,350
And I think one thing that's
useful to think about here

760
00:36:45,350 --> 00:36:49,040
is systems which you think
the Markov property might

761
00:36:49,040 --> 00:36:51,320
be violated, at least
with the features

762
00:36:51,320 --> 00:36:53,470
that you'd be using to
represent the state.

763
00:36:56,640 --> 00:36:57,370
All right.

764
00:36:57,370 --> 00:36:59,590
Now let's go on to temporal
difference learning.

765
00:36:59,590 --> 00:37:01,840
And this is, again, sort
of related to Q-learning,

766
00:37:01,840 --> 00:37:05,530
which we'll get to
in the next lecture.

767
00:37:05,530 --> 00:37:08,320
So Sutton and Barto,
which is a textbook that

768
00:37:08,320 --> 00:37:09,640
is an optional one for--

769
00:37:09,640 --> 00:37:10,140
yeah.

770
00:37:13,552 --> 00:37:14,510
I had a quick question.

771
00:37:14,510 --> 00:37:16,690
So if we don't
the rewards model,

772
00:37:16,690 --> 00:37:19,825
how do we calculate the
rewards for the trajectory?

773
00:37:19,825 --> 00:37:20,450
Great question.

774
00:37:20,450 --> 00:37:22,540
So the assumption
here is that it's

775
00:37:22,540 --> 00:37:26,290
kind of like you either
are in a real setting

776
00:37:26,290 --> 00:37:28,275
where you can sample
these from an oracle,

777
00:37:28,275 --> 00:37:30,400
or something in the real
world is giving you these.

778
00:37:30,400 --> 00:37:32,942
So you may not have an explicit
representation for the reward

779
00:37:32,942 --> 00:37:35,890
model, but you can get them, so
if your customer buys something

780
00:37:35,890 --> 00:37:38,568
or they don't, or you
have a side effect.

781
00:37:38,568 --> 00:37:40,610
So you don't necessarily
have a parametric model,

782
00:37:40,610 --> 00:37:42,590
but you are getting
real rewards.

783
00:37:42,590 --> 00:37:45,268
That's a good question.

784
00:37:45,268 --> 00:37:47,560
Anybody else have any other
questions about Monte Carlo

785
00:37:47,560 --> 00:37:51,453
before we go on to temporal
difference learning?

786
00:37:51,453 --> 00:37:53,870
And I'm going to call it just
temporal difference learning

787
00:37:53,870 --> 00:37:56,130
now, and then I'll
specify that it's actually

788
00:37:56,130 --> 00:37:59,220
TD0 for most of what
I'm going to talk about.

789
00:37:59,220 --> 00:38:06,100
So I'll just specify,
mostly discuss TD0.

790
00:38:06,100 --> 00:38:09,610
And I'll specify what I
mean by the 0 shortly.

791
00:38:09,610 --> 00:38:12,190
So Sutton and Barto, which is
one of the optional textbooks

792
00:38:12,190 --> 00:38:16,150
for the class, says, if one had
to identify one idea as central

793
00:38:16,150 --> 00:38:18,070
and novel to RL, it
would undoubtedly be

794
00:38:18,070 --> 00:38:20,110
temporal difference learning.

795
00:38:20,110 --> 00:38:24,280
And what their point is, is
that it really is sort of a way

796
00:38:24,280 --> 00:38:28,660
still to construct estimators,
both for control and for policy

797
00:38:28,660 --> 00:38:29,660
evaluation.

798
00:38:29,660 --> 00:38:32,470
And the idea is, if we think
back to that tree I showed you,

799
00:38:32,470 --> 00:38:33,970
and I'll show you
some more, there's

800
00:38:33,970 --> 00:38:37,180
going to be a way to combine
between the idea of sampling

801
00:38:37,180 --> 00:38:40,510
to approximate expectations
and bootstrapping

802
00:38:40,510 --> 00:38:43,210
to approximate future returns.

803
00:38:43,210 --> 00:38:44,800
And we'll see that in a second.

804
00:38:44,800 --> 00:38:46,630
It is model free,
meaning you don't

805
00:38:46,630 --> 00:38:49,180
need to have a parametric
representation of the reward

806
00:38:49,180 --> 00:38:51,050
function or the dynamics model.

807
00:38:51,050 --> 00:38:54,070
And the nice thing is you can
use it in episodic settings,

808
00:38:54,070 --> 00:38:56,750
or in infinite discounted
horizon settings.

809
00:38:56,750 --> 00:38:58,750
You just set off your
robot, and then it's

810
00:38:58,750 --> 00:39:02,757
just going to have to
learn to act forever.

811
00:39:02,757 --> 00:39:04,340
And one of the key
ideas is that we're

812
00:39:04,340 --> 00:39:06,980
going to update our estimates
of the value of a state

813
00:39:06,980 --> 00:39:07,875
immediately.

814
00:39:07,875 --> 00:39:09,500
So I'll put pi here
because we're still

815
00:39:09,500 --> 00:39:13,340
talking about a policy after
every single tuple of state

816
00:39:13,340 --> 00:39:16,720
action reward next state.

817
00:39:16,720 --> 00:39:19,550
So let's see how that works.

818
00:39:19,550 --> 00:39:21,380
So again, remember,
our goal is just

819
00:39:21,380 --> 00:39:24,080
to compute the expected
discounted sum of rewards

820
00:39:24,080 --> 00:39:26,330
for a particular policy.

821
00:39:26,330 --> 00:39:28,410
Now, let's think back
to the Bellman operator.

822
00:39:28,410 --> 00:39:32,010
So if we know the MDP models,
and we have a particular policy,

823
00:39:32,010 --> 00:39:35,290
we could write the Bellman
operator like that.

824
00:39:35,290 --> 00:39:37,810
And what we were doing in
incremental every visit

825
00:39:37,810 --> 00:39:40,720
Monte Carlo is we were
updating the estimate using

826
00:39:40,720 --> 00:39:44,290
one sample of the return.

827
00:39:44,290 --> 00:39:49,300
And the idea now is to say,
well, this was one sample.

828
00:39:49,300 --> 00:39:51,430
But maybe we could just
maintain-- we have access

829
00:39:51,430 --> 00:39:52,750
to a value function.

830
00:39:52,750 --> 00:39:54,580
Why couldn't we
look up and instead

831
00:39:54,580 --> 00:39:57,550
of having what the
rewards were starting

832
00:39:57,550 --> 00:40:00,190
the state till the
end of the trajectory,

833
00:40:00,190 --> 00:40:03,400
we observed a particular reward.

834
00:40:03,400 --> 00:40:06,560
We got to a
particular next state.

835
00:40:06,560 --> 00:40:08,875
Why don't we use the value
function for that state?

836
00:40:12,580 --> 00:40:15,440
So what we're doing in this
case is, instead of using G,

837
00:40:15,440 --> 00:40:17,980
we're plugging in the
immediate reward plus gamma

838
00:40:17,980 --> 00:40:20,290
times the discounted
sum of future rewards,

839
00:40:20,290 --> 00:40:22,870
using our current estimate
of the value function

840
00:40:22,870 --> 00:40:24,495
for that next state we reached.

841
00:40:29,030 --> 00:40:30,920
And here, the reason,
one of the things

842
00:40:30,920 --> 00:40:33,440
is that we don't have to wait.

843
00:40:33,440 --> 00:40:41,790
We can do this immediately,
as soon as we reach s prime.

844
00:40:41,790 --> 00:40:44,367
So as soon as we
reach s prime, as soon

845
00:40:44,367 --> 00:40:46,200
as we see the next
state, we can immediately

846
00:40:46,200 --> 00:40:47,997
update the value of
our current state.

847
00:40:47,997 --> 00:40:50,080
So we'll have to wait till
the end of the episode.

848
00:40:50,080 --> 00:40:51,997
We can use this for
infinite horizon problems.

849
00:40:56,880 --> 00:40:59,940
So this is what that looks like.

850
00:40:59,940 --> 00:41:03,647
And we're also going to
call that the TD target.

851
00:41:03,647 --> 00:41:05,730
And again, that should
look like machine learning,

852
00:41:05,730 --> 00:41:09,000
and it should look like what
we just did with Monte Carlo

853
00:41:09,000 --> 00:41:11,550
that what we're plugging
in here is we're saying,

854
00:41:11,550 --> 00:41:13,910
we're taking our old estimate.

855
00:41:13,910 --> 00:41:15,132
And we are moving it.

856
00:41:15,132 --> 00:41:17,090
We are shifting it a
little bit by our learning

857
00:41:17,090 --> 00:41:22,120
rate towards our target,
which is our reward,

858
00:41:22,120 --> 00:41:24,180
plus our discount sum
of future rewards,

859
00:41:24,180 --> 00:41:28,350
using that plug-in estimate.

860
00:41:28,350 --> 00:41:32,440
And when we think of how much
our estimate is changing,

861
00:41:32,440 --> 00:41:36,660
we often call that
the TD0 error, which

862
00:41:36,660 --> 00:41:40,110
looks at how different
is my current estimate

863
00:41:40,110 --> 00:41:42,990
of the value of a state
versus the estimate

864
00:41:42,990 --> 00:41:46,200
that I'm plugging in.

865
00:41:46,200 --> 00:41:48,190
And again, if you've
seen Q-learning before,

866
00:41:48,190 --> 00:41:50,680
this is going to look really
similar to what we had,

867
00:41:50,680 --> 00:41:52,893
but there's no max
or things like that.

868
00:41:52,893 --> 00:41:53,810
You'll see those soon.

869
00:41:58,434 --> 00:42:03,110
So the TD0 learning algorithm
just looks like the following.

870
00:42:03,110 --> 00:42:07,870
You sample a tuple of a state
action rewards next state.

871
00:42:07,870 --> 00:42:12,150
You update the value for that
starting state, and you repeat.

872
00:42:12,150 --> 00:42:17,540
And so your t goes to t plus 1,
and then you get the next tuple.

873
00:42:17,540 --> 00:42:19,540
You just do this over,
and over, and over again.

874
00:42:19,540 --> 00:42:22,860
So in our Mars Rover example,
you have state action reward,

875
00:42:22,860 --> 00:42:25,775
next state, you update, and
then you just shift along.

876
00:42:31,390 --> 00:42:33,760
Let's see what that
might look like here.

877
00:42:33,760 --> 00:42:38,790
So in this case, let's imagine
we have a policy where we always

878
00:42:38,790 --> 00:42:40,650
take action a1.

879
00:42:40,650 --> 00:42:44,700
We're going to make our discount
factor 1 to make the math easy,

880
00:42:44,700 --> 00:42:47,580
and we're going to assume
that any action from state 1

881
00:42:47,580 --> 00:42:50,750
or s7 terminates the episode.

882
00:42:50,750 --> 00:42:52,460
And then what we
see in this case is,

883
00:42:52,460 --> 00:42:54,000
we have the
following trajectory.

884
00:42:54,000 --> 00:42:57,030
We start in state s3,
we take action a1.

885
00:42:57,030 --> 00:42:58,560
We get a reward of 0.

886
00:42:58,560 --> 00:42:59,870
So this is the reward.

887
00:42:59,870 --> 00:43:03,320
We transition to
state s2, and so forth

888
00:43:03,320 --> 00:43:05,730
till the end of the episode.

889
00:43:05,730 --> 00:43:07,740
So what we would
have in this case

890
00:43:07,740 --> 00:43:12,960
is that we would make it so that
the first update we would do

891
00:43:12,960 --> 00:43:15,470
be V of s3.

892
00:43:15,470 --> 00:43:17,540
And what we would say is
that's my old estimate

893
00:43:17,540 --> 00:43:21,400
of V of s3 times 1 minus alpha.

894
00:43:21,400 --> 00:43:23,750
I've just rewritten
the above equation here

895
00:43:23,750 --> 00:43:27,770
because this was basically
1, and this is alpha times

896
00:43:27,770 --> 00:43:31,640
minus V. Plus alpha times
the immediate reward

897
00:43:31,640 --> 00:43:33,680
plus gamma times V of s2.

898
00:43:37,190 --> 00:43:39,260
So that's what that
would look like.

899
00:43:39,260 --> 00:43:44,950
And here, imagine that I've
initialized all of them

900
00:43:44,950 --> 00:43:46,600
to be 0 to start.

901
00:43:46,600 --> 00:43:50,480
So this would still
just look like 0.

902
00:43:50,480 --> 00:43:52,830
And, in fact, what
would be the only state

903
00:43:52,830 --> 00:43:55,540
I would update to not
be 0 in this episode?

904
00:44:13,810 --> 00:44:15,670
For it to be
updated, not to be 0,

905
00:44:15,670 --> 00:44:17,920
either its immediate
reward has to be 1,

906
00:44:17,920 --> 00:44:22,080
or it has to be transitioning
to a state whose value is not 0.

907
00:44:30,500 --> 00:44:31,130
Yeah.

908
00:44:31,130 --> 00:44:34,800
So what we're seeing here
is that, in this case,

909
00:44:34,800 --> 00:44:37,170
we have state action
reward next state.

910
00:44:37,170 --> 00:44:39,300
And so this is the TD update.

911
00:44:39,300 --> 00:44:41,480
And what I was saying
here is that we've

912
00:44:41,480 --> 00:44:45,230
initialized all of them to be
0, which means that, in order

913
00:44:45,230 --> 00:44:48,110
for their value to
change from being 0,

914
00:44:48,110 --> 00:44:51,830
either their immediate
reward has to be non-zero,

915
00:44:51,830 --> 00:44:55,760
or we have to transition to
a state whose value is not 0,

916
00:44:55,760 --> 00:44:58,720
because all of them,
their current value is 0.

917
00:45:05,670 --> 00:45:07,620
Were you going to guess
which state is up?

918
00:45:07,620 --> 00:45:10,140
Which one?

919
00:45:10,140 --> 00:45:14,640
Well, when you're in state
1, you have a reward 1.

920
00:45:14,640 --> 00:45:15,430
That's right.

921
00:45:15,430 --> 00:45:20,460
Yes, so you don't see any reward
here until you get to state s1.

922
00:45:20,460 --> 00:45:23,190
I'll just highlight it here.

923
00:45:23,190 --> 00:45:26,620
So at that point is when you
update your value function.

924
00:45:26,620 --> 00:45:29,910
That's the first time that you
get to anything that any reward

925
00:45:29,910 --> 00:45:31,000
becomes non-zero.

926
00:45:31,000 --> 00:45:32,970
So in that case,
what you get is s1

927
00:45:32,970 --> 00:45:38,490
is equal to V of s1, 1 minus
alpha, plus alpha times

928
00:45:38,490 --> 00:45:43,020
1 plus, gamma V of s terminal.

929
00:45:43,020 --> 00:45:45,030
s terminal is always 0.

930
00:45:45,030 --> 00:45:47,450
So it just becomes
alpha times 1.

931
00:45:50,940 --> 00:45:54,100
So why am I making you guys
do a lot of algebra here?

932
00:45:54,100 --> 00:45:57,098
I want to do it because,
if you work this out,

933
00:45:57,098 --> 00:45:58,890
and I won't go through
it here, but I think

934
00:45:58,890 --> 00:46:03,210
it's a useful exercise,
the TD episode,

935
00:46:03,210 --> 00:46:05,830
TD estimate you would
get for your whole value

936
00:46:05,830 --> 00:46:07,660
function at the
end of this episode

937
00:46:07,660 --> 00:46:10,900
is quite different than what
you get with Monte Carlo.

938
00:46:10,900 --> 00:46:14,180
So TD updates after
every single tuple,

939
00:46:14,180 --> 00:46:16,940
every single state action
reward next state tuple.

940
00:46:16,940 --> 00:46:19,490
And so that means, when you
reach the end of the episode,

941
00:46:19,490 --> 00:46:21,670
if you look at what your
value function would be,

942
00:46:21,670 --> 00:46:24,920
and I've written the value
function here just as a vector,

943
00:46:24,920 --> 00:46:26,510
but this is the value of s1.

944
00:46:26,510 --> 00:46:27,850
This is the value of s7.

945
00:46:27,850 --> 00:46:29,800
So I've just written
it as a vector.

946
00:46:29,800 --> 00:46:32,140
This is what your value
function would be.

947
00:46:32,140 --> 00:46:35,320
It would say, my current
estimate for s1 is 1,

948
00:46:35,320 --> 00:46:37,090
and everything else is 0.

949
00:46:37,090 --> 00:46:39,650
But if you look at
first visit Monte Carlo,

950
00:46:39,650 --> 00:46:41,530
it's quite different.

951
00:46:41,530 --> 00:46:44,870
And if we make gamma equal to 1
here, which I said it would be,

952
00:46:44,870 --> 00:46:48,670
it would be 1,1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0.

953
00:46:48,670 --> 00:46:49,700
Why is this?

954
00:46:49,700 --> 00:46:52,900
Because Monte Carlo waits till
the end of the episode, and then

955
00:46:52,900 --> 00:46:57,610
it uses the returns to update
any state that was visited once

956
00:46:57,610 --> 00:47:00,070
in that episode.

957
00:47:00,070 --> 00:47:02,020
And the reason that's
important is that, now,

958
00:47:02,020 --> 00:47:05,270
actually, we filled in a lot
more things because we knew.

959
00:47:05,270 --> 00:47:07,570
We observed in that
case that, not just did

960
00:47:07,570 --> 00:47:10,310
we get a reward here, but
then we saw what s2 got,

961
00:47:10,310 --> 00:47:12,680
which was also a reward
of 1 and what s3 got,

962
00:47:12,680 --> 00:47:14,890
which was also a reward of 1.

963
00:47:14,890 --> 00:47:16,300
And the reason I
bring this up is

964
00:47:16,300 --> 00:47:18,040
that there's going to be
different choices about how

965
00:47:18,040 --> 00:47:19,360
these behave,
particularly when you

966
00:47:19,360 --> 00:47:21,360
don't have a lot of data
at the beginning, which

967
00:47:21,360 --> 00:47:25,270
may be more or less data
efficient or sample efficient.

968
00:47:25,270 --> 00:47:27,803
And ideas of sample
efficiency will come up a lot.

969
00:47:27,803 --> 00:47:29,470
We'll see that a lot
later on, but we'll

970
00:47:29,470 --> 00:47:32,890
see it on Thursday, as well.

971
00:47:32,890 --> 00:47:33,390
All right.

972
00:47:33,390 --> 00:47:36,110
So what does this look
like in terms of the tree?

973
00:47:36,110 --> 00:47:38,030
So if we go back
to our tree, which

974
00:47:38,030 --> 00:47:41,540
is like expanding out potential
futures, what we can see here

975
00:47:41,540 --> 00:47:45,920
is that TD is updating the value
estimate using a sample of st

976
00:47:45,920 --> 00:47:49,490
plus 1 to approximate
an expectation.

977
00:47:49,490 --> 00:47:52,260
So in reality, if you're
doing dynamic programming,

978
00:47:52,260 --> 00:47:54,320
you would want to do
a weighted expectation

979
00:47:54,320 --> 00:47:56,450
over all the next
states you could reach,

980
00:47:56,450 --> 00:47:59,030
weighted by the probability
of getting there.

981
00:47:59,030 --> 00:48:03,560
What TD is doing is it's
just sampling one of those.

982
00:48:03,560 --> 00:48:06,380
And that sample is
an approximation

983
00:48:06,380 --> 00:48:07,730
of that expectation.

984
00:48:07,730 --> 00:48:13,820
