01:04:46,045 --> 01:04:47,045
Yeah, so this is counts.

1226
01:04:59,910 --> 01:05:00,570
Yeah.

1227
01:05:00,570 --> 01:05:04,230
It seems pretty
similar to Monte Carlo.

1228
01:05:04,230 --> 01:05:08,220
So is just the difference that
you're generating probability,

1229
01:05:08,220 --> 01:05:10,590
as opposed to calculating G?

1230
01:05:10,590 --> 01:05:11,525
Great question.

1231
01:05:11,525 --> 01:05:12,900
We're going to
hold that thought.

1232
01:05:12,900 --> 01:05:14,858
He's asking how similar
is this to Monte Carlo.

1233
01:05:14,858 --> 01:05:16,650
It's actually going to
be pretty different.

1234
01:05:16,650 --> 01:05:18,510
We're going to see
this in a second.

1235
01:05:18,510 --> 01:05:22,720
We are using our data
similar to Monte Carlo.

1236
01:05:22,720 --> 01:05:26,500
We're going to use the data
here to compute models and then

1237
01:05:26,500 --> 01:05:27,513
propagate information.

1238
01:05:27,513 --> 01:05:28,930
But they're going
to end up making

1239
01:05:28,930 --> 01:05:30,500
some interesting
different decisions.

1240
01:05:30,500 --> 01:05:31,700
So let's see that now.

1241
01:05:31,700 --> 01:05:34,150
It's a great precursor.

1242
01:05:34,150 --> 01:05:34,650
OK.

1243
01:05:34,650 --> 01:05:37,002
So let's get into batch
policy evaluation.

1244
01:05:37,002 --> 01:05:38,960
So I've said there are
these different methods.

1245
01:05:38,960 --> 01:05:41,085
They might have different
computational complexity.

1246
01:05:41,085 --> 01:05:43,570
They might be more or
less data efficient.

1247
01:05:43,570 --> 01:05:46,060
So one thing that you
might imagine doing,

1248
01:05:46,060 --> 01:05:48,850
and we'll see this a lot
shortly, a lot more next time,

1249
01:05:48,850 --> 01:05:51,820
is, well, if I
have some data, how

1250
01:05:51,820 --> 01:05:53,923
could I best use the
data that I have.

1251
01:05:53,923 --> 01:05:56,590
And this comes a lot up a lot in
the research that me and my lab

1252
01:05:56,590 --> 01:05:59,260
do, because we're often dealing
with patient data, or student

1253
01:05:59,260 --> 01:06:01,480
data, or legal data,
or others, where

1254
01:06:01,480 --> 01:06:03,910
it's really expensive to get
the data, or it's costly,

1255
01:06:03,910 --> 01:06:04,910
or it could be harmful.

1256
01:06:04,910 --> 01:06:06,670
And we want to get
as much information

1257
01:06:06,670 --> 01:06:08,620
as we can out of
the data we have.

1258
01:06:08,620 --> 01:06:10,810
So when I say batch,
what I mean is,

1259
01:06:10,810 --> 01:06:13,090
imagine that you have
a set of k episodes.

1260
01:06:13,090 --> 01:06:14,590
And now what you
want to do is you

1261
01:06:14,590 --> 01:06:19,115
want to do policy evaluation
just with that data.

1262
01:06:19,115 --> 01:06:20,740
So what we're going
to do is repeatedly

1263
01:06:20,740 --> 01:06:23,620
sample one of the episodes
that we have of those k,

1264
01:06:23,620 --> 01:06:27,052
and we're going to apply Monte
Carlo or TD0 to that episode.

1265
01:06:27,052 --> 01:06:29,260
We're just going to do that
over, and over, and over,

1266
01:06:29,260 --> 01:06:30,320
and over again.

1267
01:06:30,320 --> 01:06:32,700
And we'll see this a lot
more next time, as well.

1268
01:06:32,700 --> 01:06:35,440
And so the idea is
to just understand,

1269
01:06:35,440 --> 01:06:39,250
if we do this, given that finite
amount of data, what will Monte

1270
01:06:39,250 --> 01:06:41,620
Carlo and TD0
converge to, in terms

1271
01:06:41,620 --> 01:06:44,410
of the evaluation of the policy.

1272
01:06:44,410 --> 01:06:45,760
So let's go through that.

1273
01:06:45,760 --> 01:06:48,010
There's this really nice
example from Sutton and Barto

1274
01:06:48,010 --> 01:06:49,780
to illustrate this.

1275
01:06:49,780 --> 01:06:51,650
We have a really small domain.

1276
01:06:51,650 --> 01:06:52,678
We have two states.

1277
01:06:52,678 --> 01:06:54,470
And we're going to say
gamma is equal to 1.

1278
01:06:54,470 --> 01:06:55,810
There's no discounting.

1279
01:06:55,810 --> 01:06:58,310
And we have eight
episodes of experience.

1280
01:06:58,310 --> 01:07:05,480
So in one episode, we started in
state A, we got a reward of 0,

1281
01:07:05,480 --> 01:07:08,840
we transitioned to B, and
we got another reward of 0.

1282
01:07:08,840 --> 01:07:09,550
OK?

1283
01:07:09,550 --> 01:07:12,430
So in this case, you
can think of it as this.

1284
01:07:12,430 --> 01:07:14,980
You get a trajectory like that.

1285
01:07:14,980 --> 01:07:19,270
In some episodes, we
started in state B,

1286
01:07:19,270 --> 01:07:21,880
and we just got an
immediate reward of 1,

1287
01:07:21,880 --> 01:07:23,690
and we observed that six times.

1288
01:07:23,690 --> 01:07:26,440
So in six trajectories we just
happened to start in state B,

1289
01:07:26,440 --> 01:07:27,980
and we got a reward of 1.

1290
01:07:27,980 --> 01:07:30,850
And then in one trajectory,
we started in state B,

1291
01:07:30,850 --> 01:07:33,930
and we got our reward of 0.

1292
01:07:33,930 --> 01:07:37,710
So first, imagine if you ran
TD updates over this data

1293
01:07:37,710 --> 01:07:39,720
an infinite amount of time.

1294
01:07:39,720 --> 01:07:42,485
What do you think the
estimate of VB would be?

1295
01:07:45,630 --> 01:07:47,520
Try to remember what
it works for there,

1296
01:07:47,520 --> 01:07:52,280
as we have 1 minus alpha
times our old estimate.

1297
01:07:52,280 --> 01:07:56,510
So that's VB plus alpha
times our immediate reward,

1298
01:07:56,510 --> 01:07:58,830
plus gamma, times
the next state.

1299
01:07:58,830 --> 01:08:05,590
But here, it's just terminal
because we always terminate.

1300
01:08:05,590 --> 01:08:07,310
After B, we always terminate.

1301
01:08:07,310 --> 01:08:10,210
So you never get any future
discounted rewards in this case.

1302
01:08:10,210 --> 01:08:12,528
So what the updates look
like for TD learning

1303
01:08:12,528 --> 01:08:15,070
is that you would have 1 minus
alpha times your old estimate,

1304
01:08:15,070 --> 01:08:19,359
plus alpha times whatever
reward you get in B.

1305
01:08:19,359 --> 01:08:21,670
And imagine you just
iterate over these

1306
01:08:21,670 --> 01:08:24,529
over, and over, and over again.

1307
01:08:24,529 --> 01:08:26,029
Somebody have any
guesses of what

1308
01:08:26,029 --> 01:08:28,376
the reward would be for V of B?

1309
01:08:38,410 --> 01:08:41,460
Would it be 0.75?

1310
01:08:41,460 --> 01:08:41,960
Yeah.

1311
01:08:41,960 --> 01:08:52,990
Somebody else want to
explain now why it's 0.75?

1312
01:08:52,990 --> 01:08:55,450
The season mods?

1313
01:08:55,450 --> 01:08:58,810
Yeah, because in this case,
we had eight episodes.

1314
01:08:58,810 --> 01:09:03,710
In two of them, when we
started in B, we got 0.

1315
01:09:03,710 --> 01:09:06,670
And in six of them, we got 1.

1316
01:09:06,670 --> 01:09:09,010
So we just average
those rewards.

1317
01:09:09,010 --> 01:09:12,170
And imagine, we're just doing
this many, many, many, many,

1318
01:09:12,170 --> 01:09:14,500
many, many times.

1319
01:09:14,500 --> 01:09:18,160
So eventually, you would just
converge to this estimate

1320
01:09:18,160 --> 01:09:22,130
being 0.75.

1321
01:09:22,130 --> 01:09:24,800
What about for Monte Carlo?

1322
01:09:24,800 --> 01:09:30,890
So let's do Monte Carlo for V
of B. What would that look like?

1323
01:09:30,890 --> 01:09:32,750
So remember, for
Monte Carlo, it would

1324
01:09:32,750 --> 01:09:40,130
be 1 minus alpha times V
of B, plus alpha times G,

1325
01:09:40,130 --> 01:09:50,540
where you start in state B. Is
it going to be the same thing?

1326
01:09:50,540 --> 01:09:51,720
Is it going to be different?

1327
01:10:00,200 --> 01:10:06,320
So Monte Carlo, we're
averaging over all the returns

1328
01:10:06,320 --> 01:10:07,675
we get starting in that state.

1329
01:10:18,410 --> 01:10:19,880
Yeah.

1330
01:10:19,880 --> 01:10:24,020
So when we start at B, so
then wouldn't it be 6 over 7?

1331
01:10:24,020 --> 01:10:25,025
6 over 8.

1332
01:10:25,025 --> 01:10:26,190
6 over 8, yeah.

1333
01:10:26,190 --> 01:10:26,900
Yeah.

1334
01:10:26,900 --> 01:10:30,245
But don't we start at
A in the first one?

1335
01:10:30,245 --> 01:10:32,120
In one episode, we start
in A. But we're just

1336
01:10:32,120 --> 01:10:33,912
trying to compute the
value of B right now.

1337
01:10:33,912 --> 01:10:35,390
We'll get to A in
a second, yeah.

1338
01:10:35,390 --> 01:10:37,340
You're thinking ahead.

1339
01:10:37,340 --> 01:10:38,010
Yeah.

1340
01:10:38,010 --> 01:10:39,805
So the Monte Carlo estimate.

1341
01:10:39,805 --> 01:10:41,180
So we're just
trying to contrast.

1342
01:10:41,180 --> 01:10:42,590
So just to recap,
what we're trying

1343
01:10:42,590 --> 01:10:43,730
to do here is we're
trying to see,

1344
01:10:43,730 --> 01:10:46,070
will these two algorithms
converge to the same thing

1345
01:10:46,070 --> 01:10:46,710
or not.

1346
01:10:46,710 --> 01:10:49,085
We're going to start off and
look at what the value would

1347
01:10:49,085 --> 01:10:51,890
be of state B. In
TD, we would converge

1348
01:10:51,890 --> 01:10:56,960
to 0.75 because the immediate
reward is either 1 or 0,

1349
01:10:56,960 --> 01:11:00,830
and the discounted sum of future
woods is 0 because we terminate.

1350
01:11:00,830 --> 01:11:03,410
And in Monte Carlo
we just average over

1351
01:11:03,410 --> 01:11:05,390
all the returns we get
when we started in B,

1352
01:11:05,390 --> 01:11:09,250
and that is also 6 divided by 8.

1353
01:11:09,250 --> 01:11:12,040
All right, so now
this is the hard one.

1354
01:11:12,040 --> 01:11:12,760
OK.

1355
01:11:12,760 --> 01:11:15,610
What about of V of A?

1356
01:11:15,610 --> 01:11:19,090
What will we converge
to in these two cases?

1357
01:11:19,090 --> 01:11:21,277
So let's do Check
Your Understanding,

1358
01:11:21,277 --> 01:11:22,610
and you can respond in the poll.

