it doesn't know
that the value of A

1470
01:18:51,130 --> 01:18:53,650
has to be related
to the value of B

1471
01:18:53,650 --> 01:18:57,010
in terms of this
bootstrapping relationship.

1472
01:18:57,010 --> 01:19:00,730
But TD is making that explicit.

1473
01:19:00,730 --> 01:19:03,520
It's using the Markov decision
process to say, the value of A

1474
01:19:03,520 --> 01:19:06,760
has to exactly be equal to the
immediate reward you get in A,

1475
01:19:06,760 --> 01:19:09,530
plus gamma times the states
that I could get into,

1476
01:19:09,530 --> 01:19:12,810
which is always B,
so the value of B.

1477
01:19:12,810 --> 01:19:16,530
So TD learning is explicitly
baking that into the solution

1478
01:19:16,530 --> 01:19:18,200
you get, whereas
Monte Carlo is not.

1479
01:19:18,200 --> 01:19:20,742
Monte Carlo is just trying to
minimize the mean squared error

1480
01:19:20,742 --> 01:19:22,050
for the returns you see.

1481
01:19:22,050 --> 01:19:24,790
So they can end up giving
you very different solutions.

1482
01:19:24,790 --> 01:19:28,080
And depending on whether your
Markov property is really

1483
01:19:28,080 --> 01:19:30,185
satisfied or not, you might
want one or the other.

1484
01:19:33,200 --> 01:19:33,700
Awesome.

1485
01:19:33,700 --> 01:19:36,820
So this just summarizes quickly
some of the different properties

1486
01:19:36,820 --> 01:19:38,290
and approaches and
just highlights

1487
01:19:38,290 --> 01:19:40,960
here that temporal difference
really does exploit this Markov

1488
01:19:40,960 --> 01:19:41,470
structure.

1489
01:19:41,470 --> 01:19:43,095
And that could be
really helpful if you

1490
01:19:43,095 --> 01:19:45,880
want to leverage that to get
better estimates of earlier

1491
01:19:45,880 --> 01:19:48,610
states, like in the
case we just saw.

1492
01:19:48,610 --> 01:19:50,170
So just to summarize,
we finished

1493
01:19:50,170 --> 01:19:53,660
going through policy evaluation
with tabular settings.

1494
